# Where the app is served from. Only used to build absolute URLs in metadata —
# an Open Graph image cannot be advertised at a relative path. Defaults to the
# production domain, so a preview deploy that wants its own cards sets this.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Supabase — local dev values come from `supabase start`
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54331
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# Server-only, and never used by the app itself — this is a test credential.
# The e2e suite mints host sessions with the admin API (Google's consent screen
# cannot be driven in CI), and the db suite reads rows back past RLS with it to
# prove a blocked write really was blocked.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Google Search Console's HTML-tag token, rendered into every page's head as
# <meta name="google-site-verification">. Production only, and only if you
# verify the site by tag rather than by DNS: Google's OAuth brand verification
# requires the home page's domain to be registered to the account that owns
# the Cloud project, and a DNS TXT record on glyn.dev proves it for the
# authorized domain at the same time. Public by design — it ships in the HTML.
# Setting it takes a redeploy to appear: prerendered pages bake in the
# build-time value, and Vercel only hands a changed variable to new deploys.
GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION=

# Google sign-in. Hosts sign in with Google and guests join anonymously, so
# there is no email in the auth path at all. Create an OAuth client in Google
# Cloud with http://127.0.0.1:54331/auth/v1/callback as an authorized redirect
# URI; `supabase start` reads these two into supabase/config.toml.
#
# Not gotrue's own SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_* spelling, deliberately:
# `SUPABASE_` is reserved on the secrets API, so preview could never be given
# a value under that name — the CLI skips it and reports success. One pair of
# names serves local and preview, so both sides use these.
AUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=

# Google Maps Platform, browser side. The key powers the course builder's
# map sheet (Maps JavaScript API; referrer-restrict it to the app's
# domains — it is a different key from the server's Places key below).
# Leave it empty and the builder stays list-only. The map ID carries both
# cloud styles — cream in its light slot, Midnight in its dark slot,
# authored from the tokens in app/globals.css (masters in docs/map-styles/)
# — and the map's colorScheme picks the variant. Without it the map wears
# Google's stock look. (The legacy _CREAM/_MIDNIGHT names are still read
# for deploys configured before the two merged into one.)
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_MAP_ID=
# Server-only — never NEXT_PUBLIC. Google Cloud key with "Places API (New)"
# enabled; application restriction must be None or IP addresses (website
# restrictions block server-side calls). Without it the course builder
# degrades to add-by-name, which still works.
GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY=

# Stripe. Server key only — checkout is Stripe-hosted, so no publishable key
# exists anywhere in the app. Leave STRIPE_SECRET_KEY empty and billing is
# off entirely: no checkout, no webhook fulfilment, nothing sold. The webhook
# secret comes from the endpoint config (Dashboard → Webhooks in production;
# `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3105/api/billing/webhook` prints one
# locally). Note: the webhook route is the first production use of
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY above — a deploy that sells things needs it set.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Reporting a bug. The report screen files an issue on a PUBLIC tracker, so
# read the redaction in lib/bug-report.ts before changing what it sends.
# Server-only, never NEXT_PUBLIC: a fine-grained personal access token with
# "Issues: read and write" on that repository ALONE — nothing else, since the
# app can only ever create an issue with it. Leave it empty and reports are
# still taken: the row lands in `bug_reports` and the player is told the
# secretary has it, rather than handed a dead link.
# Setting it outside production is fine and is how the GitHub half gets
# exercised: anything Vercel does not call `production` files its issues
# titled "[preview] …" (or "[local] …"), so a test report is obvious in the
# list and `is:issue "[preview]"` sweeps them all up.
GITHUB_ISSUE_TOKEN=
# owner/repo. Defaults to the house tracker; a fork points its own here.
GITHUB_ISSUE_REPO=glynfinck/pub-golf
# Extra labels, comma-separated, on top of `bug`. Every label must already
# exist on the repository — the API refuses one it has never seen, and a
# report that 422s is filed without labels rather than lost.
GITHUB_ISSUE_LABELS=

# The caddy (AI course planning). Three doors, tried in this order, and any
# one of them is enough — none of them set and the caddy's group is not on the
# drafting table at all, the same way an absent maps key simply removes the
# map. See lib/caddy/credentials.ts, which is where the order lives.
#
#   1. AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY — Vercel's AI Gateway. An explicit choice, so it wins:
#      spend limits and a usage log, in preview as well as production. Create
#      one at vercel.com → AI Gateway → API keys.
#   2. VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN — a token Vercel mints and rotates itself, so there is
#      no secret to paste. It is NOT on by default: switch on Secure Backend
#      Access (OIDC Federation) in the project's settings first, or this door
#      is simply not there. `vercel env pull` writes one locally, valid 12h.
#   3. ANTHROPIC_API_KEY — straight to Anthropic, no gateway. What a laptop
#      uses, and the escape hatch if the gateway is ever in the way.
#
# The gateway is the preferred door on a Vercel deploy for a reason beyond
# convenience: it keeps the model spend on one bill with a cap on it, next to
# the Stripe income the green fee brings in. Both doors carry `output_config`
# (the structured card, which is what stops an invented pub) and
# `cache_control` (the prompt cache the whole cost model rests on), so the
# choice is billing and observability, not capability.
AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# The honesty box (the results-screen tip jar) is a Stripe Payment Link,
# pasted whole. Leave it empty and the box isn't on the screen — this is the
# phase-one flag. Tips grant nothing, so no webhook is involved.
NEXT_PUBLIC_HONESTY_BOX_URL=
